{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfecntAMYFt1xoYwJKz7o","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote a new model for the repair slot, `periodic_ideal_absolute_streak_penalty`, and it passes the self-check with `OK`. The previous attempt was rejected as a near-duplicate of `length_scaled_alternation_ideal`, so I dropped the length-scaling idea and changed something else.\n\n**What changed:** I refined the incumbent, `periodic_penalized_alternation_ideal`. Each person still judges a sequence by how close its switch rate is to their own ideal rate, and periodic sequences are still penalised. The one addition is a shared penalty on the longest streak, counted in flips rather than as a share of the sequence's length. Every flip beyond two in the longest run counts against randomness (HHH costs 1, HHHH costs 2). This targets the critique's second discrepancy: among pairs with the same number of switches, people pick the sequence with the shorter longest run more often than the incumbent predicts. That one didn't survive the FDR correction (q = 0.104). The streak counted in flips also grows with length, so it may partly address the length-slope discrepancy, which did survive.\n\n**Why it should differ from the live models:**\n- `personal_alternation_ideal_streak_penalty` scales its streak penalty by sequence length and has no periodic penalty.\n- `length_scaled_alternation_ideal` (what the previous attempt duplicated) changes how strongly the distance from the ideal counts, not the streaks.\n\n**Check result:** the smoke fit's ELPD-LOO was −1073.1, against the incumbent's −1072.0. So far that is essentially a tie. A 100-draw, single-chain fit is not a real comparison, though, and the extra parameter may not earn its place.\n\nTwo warnings came with the check:\n- **Convergence not verified:** with one chain there is no R-hat. Admission's full fit decides whether it meets the convergence limits.\n- **PSIS-